# jarvis-primary.Modelfile
#
#   ollama create jarvis-primary -f jarvis-primary.Modelfile
#
# Target: RTX 2080 Super, 8 GB GDDR6, Turing sm75 (compute capability 7.5),
# Windows 11. Usable ceiling ~6.90 GiB once DWM and the Tauri shell have taken
# their ~1.1 GiB.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
# A local chat turn posts to OLLAMA_URL/v1/chat/completions, Ollama's
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint (ollama-direct.patch; since chat-stream.patch,
# through jarvis_agent.run_local_turn). It sends model, messages, stream,
# temperature, max_tokens, reasoning_effort and, with tools on, tools. No
# num_ctx, no options: the OpenAI compat surface has no field for num_ctx - so
# there is no request that could set it. The context length therefore has to
# be set on the MODEL, which is this file, or in the environment.
# (jarvis_agent does ask Ollama what the loaded model has, and trims the
# history to fit - that avoids silent cutting; it cannot make room.)
#
# Left alone, Ollama picks. server/sched.go:
#
#     func nextLowerAutoNumCtx(numCtx int) (int, bool) {
#         switch {
#         case numCtx > 32768: return 32768, true
#         case numCtx > 4096:  return 4096, true
#         default:             return 0, false
#         }
#     }
#
# An 8B Q4 at 32768 with an f16 cache needs about 9.8 GiB, so it does not fit,
# so the ladder drops to 4096 - and stops. **The deployed context today is
# 4096 tokens.** A 6,000-token prompt does not fit in it. Every context number
# anyone has planned around on this machine has been hypothetical.
#
# THE BUDGET, arithmetic rather than assertion
#   weights  Q4_K_M   8.190B params x 4.90 bpw / 8          = 4.67 GiB
#   KV       q8_0     2 x 36 layers x 8 kv_heads x 128 head_dim
#                       x 1.0625 B/elem x 16384 tokens      = 1.19 GiB
#   runtime  CUDA context ~330 MiB + compute buffer @ nb512 = 0.63 GiB
#                                                             --------
#                                                              6.48 GiB of 6.90
#
# REQUIRED IN THE ENVIRONMENT - a Modelfile cannot express these:
#
#   OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE=q8_0
#       Without it the KV term is 2.25 GiB and this configuration SPILLS into
#       system RAM, where it runs at roughly a fifth of the speed.
#
#   OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=-1
#       Ollama's default is 5 minutes. With one resident model there is
#       nothing an idle timer can make room FOR; its only effect is to
#       guarantee a ~6 second stall on the first word after you step away.
#       Set it in the environment rather than per-request so the extractor's
#       /api/generate calls in jarvis_extract inherit it too.
#
#   Do NOT set OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION, to 0 or to 1.
#       Auto is correct. Forcing 1 removes llama.cpp's per-model fallback.
#
# VERIFY ONCE, BEFORE TRUSTING ANY OF THIS
# Everything above depends on flash attention being live, because llama.cpp
# reaches the q8_0 KV path only through its fused-attention kernels. The gate
# is ml/device.go:
#
#     func cudaFlashAttentionSupported(gpu DeviceInfo) bool {
#         if gpu.Library != "CUDA" || gpu.ComputeMajor < 6 ||
#            (gpu.ComputeMajor == 7 && gpu.ComputeMinor == 2) { return false }
#         ...
#         return gpu.DriverMajor >= 7
#     }
#
# The 2080 Super is compute capability 7.5. The only 7.x excluded is 7.2,
# which is Jetson Xavier. So it should pass - but read that as "should".
# Confirm it on the machine:
#
#     $env:OLLAMA_DEBUG=1; ollama run jarvis-primary "hi"
#
# and grep the llama-server argv in the log for `--flash-attn`. If it reads
# `off`, q8_0 KV is being silently ignored, this file spills, and the fix is
# either num_ctx 8192 here or the qwen2.5:7b fallback described at the bottom.
#
# (Note for anyone reading the older advice: "FlashAttention-2 does not
# support Turing" is true of Dao-AILab's flash-attn PyPI package, which needs
# sm80+. Ollama does not use it. llama.cpp has its own CUDA fused-attention
# kernels and GGML_CUDA_CC_TURING is 750.)

FROM qwen3:8b

# ---- context -------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole point of the file. See nextLowerAutoNumCtx above.
PARAMETER num_ctx 16384

# 512, pinned, and this is not a tuning preference. server/sched.go:
#
#     func generationBatchSurcharge(batch int) uint64 {
#         switch {
#         case batch >= llamaServerGenerationBatchLarge:   // 2048
#             return 2 * format.GibiByte
#         case batch >= llamaServerGenerationBatchMedium:  // 1024
#             return 768 * format.MebiByte
#         default:
#             return 0
#         }
#     }
#
# Ollama's auto-batch reaches for 1024 or 2048 when it thinks there is
# headroom. Either one turns 6.48 GiB into a spill, and nothing tells you it
# happened except that everything got slow.
PARAMETER num_batch 512

# A GENERATION budget, spent out of the same window the conversation and the
# recalled facts need. jarvis_agent sends max_tokens 1024 when an app asks for
# nothing - this same number - so every window gets the same length of answer
# (the HUD page used to ask for 2048). jarvis_persona's own
# max_response_tokens is 900.
PARAMETER num_predict 1024

# ---- sampling ------------------------------------------------------------
# Qwen's documented NON-THINKING values. Thinking is meant to be off. Nothing
# in this file switches it off, and Ollama switches it ON by default for a
# model that can think - so it is switched off on every request instead:
# jarvis_agent sends reasoning_effort "none" (Ollama's supported switch on this
# endpoint) and cuts any <think> block out of the answer as well
# (chat-stream.patch). Until that patch is applied, thinking is ON. Why off:
# a <think> block ahead of the content makes barge-in impossible in the voice
# loop - time to first USEFUL token is what matters, not time to first token -
# and jarvis_extract's schema-constrained calls spend the whole generation
# fighting a grammar that forbids the block the model wants to emit first.
#
# For visible reasoning, derive a second model from this one rather than
# changing these: temperature 0.6, top_p 0.95, num_predict 3072, think on.
PARAMETER temperature 0.7
PARAMETER top_p 0.8
PARAMETER top_k 20
PARAMETER min_p 0.0
PARAMETER repeat_penalty 1.05

# ---- stop tokens ---------------------------------------------------------
# ChatML. <|im_start|> is listed because a model that has started hallucinating
# the next turn has finished this one, and jarvis_structured.parse would
# otherwise be handed two JSON objects and reject the pair.
PARAMETER stop "<|im_end|>"
PARAMETER stop "<|im_start|>"

# ---- the invariants ------------------------------------------------------
# This block reaches the model only when messages[0] is not already a system
# message. ollama/server/routes.go:
#
#     msgs := append(m.Messages, req.Messages...)
#     if req.Messages[0].Role != "system" && m.System != "" {
#         msgs = append([]api.Message{{Role: "system", Content: m.System}}, msgs...)
#     }
#
# Before memory-prefix.patch the HUD prepended the recalled-facts block, so
# messages[0] WAS a system message on every turn where recall fired, and this
# block was silently dropped at exactly the moment the model was holding the
# owner's private facts. With that patch applied the facts go in next to the
# question instead and this survives. If you are running an unpatched
# jarvis_hud.py, these invariants are not reaching the model on retrieval
# turns, whatever this file says.
SYSTEM """You are Jarvis, a private assistant running entirely on this machine.

Say what is a guess and what is verified. If you are not sure, say you are not sure - a confident wrong answer costs more here than a hedged one.

Never claim an action was taken that was not. You do not send email, edit files, or run commands yourself; you propose them and a person approves each one. If you have proposed something, say that you have proposed it, not that it is done.

Anything recalled about the owner is private and stays on this machine. Do not repeat it back unless it is relevant to what was asked.
"""
